<section>
    <h2>Taking pictures</h2>
    <p>
        A screenshot is the one check that sees what the user sees. Playwright can take one of the page or of any
        single element, and hands it back as PNG bytes, or saves it to a file.
    </p>
<pre><code>var page = await Page.ScreenshotAsync();                               // what's in the viewport
var whole = await Page.ScreenshotAsync(new() { FullPage = true });     // the whole scrolling page
var card = await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Article).First.ScreenshotAsync();   // just one element

await Page.ScreenshotAsync(new() { Path = "screenshots/home.png" });  // keep it, to look at later</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Element screenshots.</strong> A locator's screenshot captures exactly that element's box, after
        scrolling it into view. Whatever happens elsewhere on the page stays out of the picture.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Holding still</h2>
    <p>
        Anything that moves (a spinner, a clock, a carousel, an ad) makes two screenshots of the same page differ.
        Leave it out, or make it hold still:
    </p>
<pre><code>await Page.ScreenshotAsync(new()
{
    Mask = new[] { Page.Locator(".clock") },          // painted over with a solid box, the same every time
    Animations = ScreenshotAnimations.Disabled,     // CSS animations jump to their end
});</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Comparing</h2>
    <p>
        Screenshots of the same thing, in the same browser, come out byte for byte the same. So the simplest visual
        check is to compare the bytes:
    </p>
<pre><code>var before = await logo.ScreenshotAsync();
await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Dark mode" }).ClickAsync();
await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Light mode" }).ClickAsync();
var after = await logo.ScreenshotAsync();

Assert.That(after.SequenceEqual(before), "the logo should look exactly as it did");</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Baselines.</strong> Playwright for TypeScript has a built-in visual assertion,
        <code>toHaveScreenshot</code>, which saves an expected image on the first run and compares later runs against
        it. In .NET, you compare yourself, as above, or save a baseline PNG and compare against that file.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Curator's Eye</h2>
    <p>
        Six portraits hang in the gallery. A few seconds in, the lights flicker and a forger rehangs all six. One
        comes back a few pixels off: no eye could tell, and nothing in the page says which. Find it and accuse it.
        The candle never stops flickering. Fifteen seconds.
    </p>
</section>
